An HTTP 429 'Too Many Requests' response isn't a pipeline failure; it's simply a server politely asking you to slow down. 
Treating rate limits as fatal exceptions is the fastest way to build a fragile data pipeline that crashes at the slightest hint of network traffic.

Technical Breakdown :- 
To handle this mathematically without crashing, we use a strategy called Exponential Backoff with Jitter. 
If an API rejects our request, we don't blindly try again or wait a static 60 seconds. Instead, we pause for a short duration (like 2 seconds) and retry. 
If it fails again, we double the wait to 4 seconds, then 8, then 16, capping at a maximum threshold.
Crucially, we inject 'Jitter'—a small randomized time variance (e.g., 4.3 seconds instead of exactly 4.0). 
This ensures that if ten parallel worker nodes get rate-limited simultaneously, they don't all wake up at the exact 
same millisecond and accidentally DDoS the target server in a 'thundering herd'.

Buisness Context :- 
Enterprise platforms like Salesforce, Shopify, or Zendesk strictly throttle their API endpoints.
If your nightly ingestion job crashes due to a temporary 5-second traffic spike, downstream financial reports will be missing critical
revenue data at 8 AM. Graceful rate-limit handling guarantees that the pipeline bends instead of breaks, ensuring 100% data completeness without manual engineering intervention.

Design Decision :- 
engineers usually clutter their extraction scripts with messy while loops and manual time.sleep() commands.
As a data engineer, I abstract this away using Python decorators from the tenacity or backoff libraries, keeping the core logic completely clean. 
Furthermore, if I am running 50 distributed Airflow workers hitting the same API, local exponential backoff isn't enough. 
I architect a centralized 'Token Bucket' using Redis, forcing all 50 workers to draw from a globally shared pool of API tokens,
strictly guaranteeing we never breach the provider's rate limit in the first place.

Note:- How centralized token bucket in redis works?
In a distributed data architecture, local rate limiting is blind. If you have 50 Airflow worker nodes and an external API limit of 10 requests per second,
a worker has no idea how many requests the other 49 nodes are making. If they all fire at once, you hit 50 requests and get blocked.

To prevent this, we use a centralized, blazing-fast in-memory database like Redis to act as the 'Token Bucket'.

The Bucket: We configure Redis to hold a maximum of 10 digital 'tokens'.

The Refill: A background process refills this bucket at a strict rate of 10 tokens every second.

The Check: Before any worker anywhere in the cluster fires an API request, it must ping Redis and grab a token. 
If the bucket is empty (0 tokens), the worker pauses locally and waits for the next refill. The external API is completely shielded from the chaos.
